WEBVTT


00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.610
Do you want your sweaters to fit better?

00:00:03.030 --> 00:00:04.290
Ad bust darts.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:06.600
Are you larger than an "A" cup?

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.350
Add bust darts.

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.080
Do you tend to yank down on the fronts
of your sweaters when you wear them?

00:00:13.510 --> 00:00:14.920
Add bust darts.

00:00:16.390 --> 00:00:21.550
Consider adding them to your next sweater
even if your pattern doesn't include them,

00:00:22.150 --> 00:00:25.480
you'll be pleasantly
surprised at the improved fit.

00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.450
Here are some things to consider.

00:00:31.495 --> 00:00:37.625
Our goal is to add some length to the
center front of your sweater so that the

00:00:37.625 --> 00:00:39.895
hem hangs parallel to the floor.

00:00:41.385 --> 00:00:46.485
Use your measurements to determine if you
need one inch or three inches or more.

00:00:49.170 --> 00:00:52.440
Where to add them is the
next thing to consider.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.350
Where should the dart start?

00:00:56.190 --> 00:01:00.330
You want the dart to point
to the apex of your bust.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.300
So measure down from your shoulder where
your shoulder seam would be to determine

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.980
where to start your shaping.

00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.940
Generally you'll knit the dart
before the armhole shaping.

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:19.164
The Knit it Now patterns position the
darts one inch before the armhole shaping.

00:01:19.975 --> 00:01:23.544
But measure to see where
this would be best for you.

00:01:25.645 --> 00:01:27.655
How deep should the dart be?

00:01:30.145 --> 00:01:35.035
The rule of thumb is that bust darts
should end approximately two inches from

00:01:35.035 --> 00:01:36.685
the apex of your bust.

00:01:36.955 --> 00:01:42.475
This gives a nice soft,
flattering curve to the fabric.

00:01:43.845 --> 00:01:46.935
Measure from your side seam to this point.

00:01:48.015 --> 00:01:52.845
Before we get involved with any math,
let's talk a little about the actual

00:01:52.845 --> 00:01:54.135
knitting of darts.

00:01:55.125 --> 00:01:56.145
On the knitting machine

00:01:56.145 --> 00:01:57.555
this is pretty straight forward.

00:01:58.195 --> 00:02:02.515
We will work short row shaping
or also called partial knitting.

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:08.525
We're going to add more rows to the center
front of the garment piece without adding

00:02:08.525 --> 00:02:10.305
any length to the side seams.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:16.650
Now, if you aren't familiar with short
rows or holding and wrapping, please look

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.079
for the tutorials in our learning library.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.780
Basically, you set your
carriage to the hold setting.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:25.750
And you pull groups of
needles to hold position.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.055
In this example, I've changed to a
contrast in color as I started forming my

00:02:32.055 --> 00:02:34.575
dirt shaping to make a little more clear.

00:02:35.595 --> 00:02:40.545
I'm adding a single dart on one side of
the work as if I were knitting a cardigan

00:02:40.545 --> 00:02:40.965
front.

00:02:42.345 --> 00:02:46.995
Following my pattern, I'm putting a group
of needles that are opposite the carriage

00:02:47.025 --> 00:02:48.585
into hold position.

00:02:49.425 --> 00:02:56.425
I'll knit across and manually wrap that
first needle to prevent a hole and knit

00:02:56.445 --> 00:02:56.895
back.

00:02:57.975 --> 00:03:03.435
I'll keep repeating this according again,
according to my pattern and notice how the

00:03:03.435 --> 00:03:05.985
white section is getting longer.

00:03:06.555 --> 00:03:09.225
This is going to be the
center front of my sweater.

00:03:10.725 --> 00:03:15.135
In this example, I've worked stripes so
you can see the shaping a little more

00:03:15.135 --> 00:03:15.765
clearly.

00:03:17.625 --> 00:03:22.394
Notice that between the markers is the
additional length that I've added that

00:03:22.394 --> 00:03:27.435
will accomplish our goal of adding
inches to the center front of my garment.

00:03:28.214 --> 00:03:31.665
Look closely at a solid
color and plain knitting.

00:03:32.114 --> 00:03:33.135
The wrapped stitches.

00:03:33.135 --> 00:03:35.685
The short rows really
aren't that noticeable.

00:03:38.954 --> 00:03:42.285
I've determined that I want
my dart to start at this row.

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:50.130
I need two inches of additional length and
I want my dart to be four inches in from

00:03:50.130 --> 00:03:50.920
the side seam.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:57.540
Now I could get out my calculator and use
the magic formula and do bunch of math,

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.050
but why not just use the
Knit it Now dart tool?

00:04:01.770 --> 00:04:03.450
Let us do the math for you.

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.150
Fill in the blanks.

00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:12.405
Click.And voila.

00:04:13.185 --> 00:04:15.075
Here are your shaping instructions.

00:04:16.875 --> 00:04:21.315
This tool is free for Knit it Now,
subscribers, and it's included in our

00:04:21.315 --> 00:04:25.034
dynamic patterns that offer
optional bust dart shaping.

00:04:27.635 --> 00:04:31.775
Adding bust darts will improve
the fit of your sweaters.

00:04:32.075 --> 00:04:33.125
Give them a try.

00:04:33.605 --> 00:04:36.725
I'm sure you will be
pleased with the results.

00:04:38.705 --> 00:04:39.635
Thanks for watching.

